<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zanj</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zanj was the name that medieval Arabic and Persian writers gave to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> peoples of the East African coast, which they called the land of the Zanj. Many Zanj were carried as slaves to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, where those forced to clear the salt marshes near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> rose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj-rebellion">Zanj Rebellion</a> of 869 to 883. The name survives in Zanzibar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zanj.jpg" alt="Zanj" width="320" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Runehelmet · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Swahili_coast.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
